        h2 §The Rust core allocation and collections library @149,79,562,21
        text This library provides smart pointers and collections for managing heap-allocated values. @149,112,562,41
        text This library, like core, normally doesn’t need to be used directly since its contents are re-exported in the . Crates that use the attribute however will typically not depend on , so they’d use this crate instead. @149,165,562,61
        link "std crate" ../std/index.html @149,238,562,20
        text #![no_std] @149,270,562,20
        text std @149,302,562,21
        h3 §Boxed values @149,335,562,18
        text The type is a smart pointer type. There can only be one owner of a , and the owner can decide to mutate the contents, which live on the heap. @149,365,562,41
        row @149,418,562,20
          link Box boxed/index.html @149,418,26,20
          link Box boxed/index.html @187,418,26,20
        text This type can be sent among threads efficiently as the size of a Box value is the same as that of a pointer. Tree-like data structures are often built with boxes because each node often has only one owner, the parent. @149,450,562,61
        h3 §Reference counted pointers @149,523,562,19
        text The type is a non-threadsafe reference-counted pointer type intended for sharing memory within a thread. An pointer wraps a type, , and only allows access to , a shared reference. @149,554,562,61
        link Rc rc/index.html @149,627,562,20
        link Rc rc/index.html @149,659,562,20
        text T @149,691,562,21
        text &T @149,724,562,20
        text This type is useful when inherited mutability (such as using ) is too constraining for an application, and is often paired with the or types in order to allow mutation. @149,756,562,41
        link Box boxed/index.html @149,809,562,20
